  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "In a video game, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $80$ and $125,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability of occurring, what is the standard deviation of the number of obstacles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =125-80+1 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{46}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2\\,115}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,115}{12}}≈13.28.\n$$",
